Apple could unveil its long-rumored smart home display as soon as October, according to <em>Bloomberg</em>'s <a href="https://www.bloomberg.com/news/newsletters/2026-09-20/apple-s-home-ai-hub-details-apple-fitness-layoffs-and-iphone-duo-apple-pencil-mu9vv9k0">Mark Gurman</a>.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2025/08/Home-Hub-Command-Center-with-Dome-Base-Feature.jpg" alt="" width="2000" height="1125" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-1014641" /><br/>
In the <a href="https://www.bloomberg.com/news/newsletters/2026-09-20/apple-s-home-ai-hub-details-apple-fitness-layoffs-and-iphone-duo-apple-pencil-mu9vv9k0">latest edition</a> of his "Power On" newsletter, Gurman narrows Apple's launch window after he said in August that the smart home display remained on track to <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/08/25/apple-smart-home-display-still-coming/">arrive before the end of 2026</a>. The device is expected to become a central interface for controlling accessories, communicating with other people in the home, accessing media, and using Apple services.<br/>
<br/>
Apple is reportedly testing the product extensively in employees' homes. References found in Apple's software have linked the tabletop model to the internal designation "J490," while a second model identified as "J491" appears to be intended for wall mounting. Both identifiers were among several unreleased products <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/08/17/macos-26-7-unreleased-apple-devices/">referenced in macOS Tahoe 26.7</a>.<br/>
<br/>
The two versions will reportedly share a compact, square display. One will include a base for placing it on a desk or counter, while the other can be fixed to a wall. The screen is expected to be smaller than those used by many Amazon Echo Show models.<br/>
<br/>
Apple has apparently developed a dedicated operating system built around <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/siri/">Siri</a> AI for the product, with visual elements drawn from iOS, tvOS, and watchOS. Its interface will offer a <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/home-screen/">Home Screen</a>, widgets, and selectable clock designs, allowing information to remain visible when the device is not being actively used. Users will reportedly be able to search information held across their Apple devices and accounts with &zwnj;Siri&zwnj; AI, retrieve previously shared songs or podcasts, locate photos, and request specific actions within apps.<br/>
<br/>
Apple is believed to have postponed the home hub several times because these &zwnj;Siri&zwnj; features were not ready. That obstacle has now largely been removed following the <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/09/14/apple-releases-ios-27/">release of iOS 27 with Siri AI</a> earlier this month.<br/>
<br/>
The display's camera will purportedly support video calls and recognize individual members of a household. Once it identifies the person in front of it, the software will be able to present that user's appointments, notes, preferred media, news, and other personalized information. The camera system reportedly provides this recognition without the depth-sensing components used for <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/mask-face-id/">Face ID</a>.<br/>
<br/>
Other expected functions include smart home controls, intercom communication, music and video playback, and access to apps such as Calendar, Notes, and Reminders. An iPhone will be required during setup and for certain connected features.<br/>
<br/>
The home display would be among the first major new products introduced under Apple CEO John Ternus. Gurman says Apple intends for the iPhone to remain the center of customers' mobile lives, while the display, <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/apple-tv/">Apple TV</a>, and <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/homepod/">HomePod</a> establish a corresponding hub for the home.<div class="linkback">Tags: <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/apple-command-center/">Apple Command Center</a>, <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/bloomberg-com/">Bloomberg</a>, <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/mark-gurman/">Mark Gurman</a></div><br/>This article, &quot;<a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/09/21/apple-smart-home-display-coming-next-month/">Apple Smart Home Display Coming as Soon as Next Month</a>&quot; first appeared on <a href="https://www.macrumors.com">MacRumors.com</a><br/><br/><a href="https://forums.macrumors.com/threads/apple-smart-home-display-coming-as-soon-as-next-month.2490087/">Discuss this article</a> in our forums<br/><br/>